content:
liam: FYI onboarding for VelvetCart hit a spike of 429s on preflight during demo. Their Electron app spawns multiple renderers + an updater process that all call sdk.init() on resume. Repro in ~15s after resume.

liam: logs: ```
[2026-04-12T09:40:11Z] renderer-1 init /v1/preflight 200
[2026-04-12T09:40:12Z] renderer-2 init /v1/preflight 429 x 14
[2026-04-12T09:40:12Z] updater mint /v1/token 429 x 7
[2026-04-12T09:40:13Z] renderer-3 init /v1/preflight 429 x 9
```

nina: Which SDK version and auth pattern? Shared server key or ephemeral/session tokens?

liam: Browser/Electron SDK v1.8.2 embedded into each renderer. For speed they wired a single dev API key into the app during onboarding (server key copied into installers). No splay or cross-process guards.

arun: That's a classic fanout across both preflight and token endpoints. Two immediate tracks: customer-side quick fixes and ops-side short-term smoothing.

arun: Customer-side guidance to send now: 1) stop using the long-lived server key in shipped builds; mint short-lived session tokens from their backend per renderer/process, 2) add randomized init splay and an inflight guard per-origin/process, 3) for Electron, use app.requestSingleInstanceLock() or an OS-level temp-file lock so only one process mints at a time. Example Electron pattern:```js
// main.js
const gotLock = app.requestSingleInstanceLock();
if (!gotLock) { app.quit(); }
// in renderer: simple process-level guard
if (window.__VC_init) return;
const delay = 200 + Math.floor(Math.random()*900);
setTimeout(()=>{ if (window.__VC_init) return; window.__VC_init=true; sdk.init().finally(()=>window.__VC_init=false); }, delay);
// minting: renderer calls backend /session-token which mints a 60s token
```

arun: For token mints: recommend backend endpoint that returns a token TTL=60s. If they can't change quickly, offer a tiny mint-proxy pattern (coalescing):```js
let pending = null;
async function mintCoalesce(){ if (pending) return pending; pending = fetch('/internal/mint',{method:'POST'}).then(r=>r.json()).finally(()=> pending=null); return pending; }
```

liam: I'm going to send the above snippets. Anything we can flip on our side for a fast reduction?

arun: Yes. Ops-side options: 1) enable tenant preflight_coalesce for "velvetcart", 2) temporarily increase preflight burst allowance by +160 tokens with TTL=60m, 3) add a 2.5s leaky-bucket smoothing window on both /v1/preflight and /v1/token for that tenant. Those will blunt immediate 429s while they roll client changes.

ratebot: RateLimitAlert: tenant="velvetcart" detected spike 2026-04-12T09:40:10Z..2026-04-12T09:45:00Z peak_preflight=158 req/min peak_mint=82 req/min. Suggested actions: preflight_coalesce=true, burst_window_inc=+160, token_leaky_window=2500ms.

nina: I'll ask for infra change with the flags/ttl. Liam, confirm with customer if they can accept a 1-hour maintenance window to flip to ephemeral tokens. If yes, schedule it and we'll apply the ops patch right before.

liam: Customer says they can do a 30-minute window starting in ~30m. I'll schedule and share the init + mint snippets.

arun: Post-rollout checklist: 1) monitor 429s for 2 hours, 2) collect 8 init traces across renderers, 3) sample 5 token mint traces and ensure token header differs per process, 4) verify installer doesn't contain the long-lived server key. If 429s persist after client splay and ephemeral tokens, escalate to runtime team for KV cache/coalescing kernel analysis.

liam: Adding SE and infra contacts to the ticket and will update after the window. Thanks all :thumbsup:
